:A narrative can be made more vivid by using different kinds of past tenses.The examples given here are from a biography.

The simple past (verb+ed) is used to describe events that happened in the past in chronological order.

<h3 class="ex">Example</h3><div class="exemple">
The following year he ??married,had married,marry?? Jacqueline Bouvier who
??bore,was bearing,bear?? him 4
children in the following years. However only ??two survived,was surviving,had survived to infancy??: Caroline and John.
</div>

The past continuous tense <span class="gram">be+ verb+ing</span> is used to describe
a longer action that is interrupted by a shorter action.

<h3 class="ex">Example</h3><div class="exemple">
Kennedy was killed while he ??was riding,rode,ride??
in an open car.
</div>
<span class="gram">while he was riding' </span> = while riding

<p>
When the subject of the two verbs is identical the auxiliary can be left out and
an elliptical structure is thus created.

The past perfect tense (<span class="gram">Had + past participle of the verb</span>)
is used when an event happened before another past event.
</p>
<h3 class="ex">Example</h3><div class="exemple">John Fitzgerald Kennedy
??was born,born,had born,is born?? in Brookline, Massachusetts, on May
29, 1917. Kennedy's family ??had emigrated,was emigrating,emigrated??
from Ireland in 1849.
</div>
The past perfect is sometimes used to improve style : to show part of the story in a flashback.
Certain words in the text may indicate that the event had happened earlier (for example 'before'),
in which case the use of the past perfect is not necessary.

<h3 class="ex">Example</h3><div class="exemple">
Khrushchev ordered his ships to turn back before they reached the Navy
</div>
The past perfect tense can be used with adverbs such as
<span class="gram">barely, hardly... when, no sooner ..than</span>
to describe en event that had been completed just before another one happened.
 With "barely", "hardly ...when" the verb and the subject can be inverted, with "no sooner ...than" inversion of the subject and verb is obligatory .

<h3 class="ex">Example</h3><div class="exemple">
He had barely had time to work on the civil rights bill as he was assassinated
in Dallas ...
 <p> Hardly had he finished when his friend arrived.</p>

 <p>No sooner ??had Peter received,Peter had received?? the letter than he rung her.</p>
</div>

The past perfect tense is the equivalent of the present perfect tense in a past context.

<h3 class="ex">Example</h3><div class="exemple">
 ... the first Roman Catholic president who ??had ever been,has ever been?? elected.
</div>

<p>
Simple past passive : <span class="gram">was/were +past participle</span>
Past perfect passive : <span class="gram">had been +past participle</span>
</p>
<h3 class="ex">Example</h3><div class="exemple">
Because he ??had been injured,was being injured,had injured??,
he ??was sent,had sent,was sending?? back to the USA ...
</div>
<span class="gram">
would +verb </span> form is used to describe a future event in a past context. This structure is also called the "future in the past".

<h3 class="ex">Example</h3><div class="exemple">
This confrontation ??would mark,will mark,was marking?? a change in the development of the Cold War.
</div>

<span class="gram">would+verb</span> can also refer to indirect speech.
<h3 class="ex">Example</h3><div class="exemple">
He told me he ??wouldn't go,will not go?? to that party.
</div>

<span class="gram">was/were to + verb</span>
is used to describe an event that necessarily had to happen later in the future.
It has a similar meaning to "would+verb".

<h3 class="ex">Example</h3><div class="exemple">
In 1961, he ??was to become,was becoming?? the first Roman Catholic
president ...</div>
